  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and seasonable weather continues through the\nweek. The first day of November could bring some rain.\n\nHigh pressure remains more or less over the Mid-Atlantic and \nthe Southeastern states through the end of the month. The ridge \nwill flatten out some in response to short wave energy moving \nthrough the faster flow to our north. Mid and upper level \nmoisture will increase by midweek, but the next chance of rain \nwill not come until a southern stream disturbance opens up and \nlifts towards the Mid-Atlantic around Halloween night \n(Saturday). With limited moisture and unorganized forcing, we \nmay only see some spotty showers begin to develop overnight into\nthe day after Halloween (Sunday). Other than increasing clouds,\nwe should have good trick-or-treat weather with mild\ntemperatures. Locations that miss out on that rain will have \nto wait until mid week to get another shot. \n\nWe may see some patchy frost tonight into Tuesday morning,\notherwise expect the normal valley fog. Temperatures will be a\nfew degrees within normal values, with highs in the 60s and 70s\nareawide, and lows in the 40s and 50s (save for tonight).",
